<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's the Best! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Best!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage in simple exchanges about personal interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a current personal experience related to familiar places in sequence when sharing with peers (e.g., 'I am at the park, and I see trees.'). Can engage in simple exchanges about personal interests and listen to peers' preferences (e.g., 'I like the park because it is fun. What do I like?'). Can express opinions about a familiar place (e.g., 'The park is the best because it has swings.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the communicative goal.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different neighborhood places (park, street, building, playground, flower, tree). Teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and "Do you like the park?" using simple yes/no answers or 1-2 word responses. Then, the teacher says, "Today we will talk about our favorite places."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a flashcard of a park and says, "I like the park. It is fun! The park is the best." The teacher then points to a flashcard of a playground and says, "I like the playground because it has swings." Teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in 'best' and /f/ in 'favorite', asking students to repeat chorally. The teacher also shows how to blend /b/ and /ɪg/ to form 'big', 'bigger', and 'biggest' by saying them clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'the best' good or bad?" (Expected answer: Good!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I like the street because it has beautiful trees,' am I saying why I like it?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated short dialogue between two friends, Leo and Mia, titled "Our Favorite Spots."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Hi, Mia! I like to walk in our neighborhood."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mia: "Me too, Leo! What is your favorite place?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "I like the park. It is the best because it has big trees and flowers!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mia: "Oh, I see. I like the playground. It is my favorite because it is fun! I ride the swings there."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "That's cool! I sometimes play at the playground too."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret short, simple dialogues about familiar topics, identifying key information about preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud, then plays an audio recording (if available). Students listen.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Are Leo and Mia talking about school or their neighborhood?" (Expected answer: Neighborhood!) "Do they like their neighborhood?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Mia's favorite place is the park." (False) "Leo likes the park because it has big trees." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Superlatives ('the best'), Simple present + conjunction ('because'), Question structure ('What is your favorite?'). Vocabulary: park, playground, street, building, tree, flower, fun, exciting, beautiful, favorite, best.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Builder</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher writes sentence starters on the board or provides a worksheet. Students complete the sentences orally in chorus, then individually. E.g.:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The [park] is the _____." (best/worst)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like the [street] _____ it has beautiful [trees]." (because)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The [playground] is my [favorite] _____ it is [fun]." (because)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher emphasizes pronunciation of /b/ and /f/ and the blend /bɪg/ for 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest'.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Substitution Drill (Pair Work)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Teacher provides sentence frames and a list of vocabulary (places: park, street, playground, building; adjectives: fun, exciting, beautiful, quiet, amazing). Partner A says: "I like the (place) because it is (adjective)." Partner B responds: "Yes, it is (adjective)! I think the (place) is the (best/worst)." Students take turns using different words from the list.</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example: Student A: "I like the park because it is beautiful." Student B: "Yes, it is beautiful! I think the park is the best."</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interview</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students interview their partner using target questions. Teacher writes the questions on the board:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite place in the neighborhood?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do you like it?" (Encourage using 'because').</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice asking and answering these questions with their partner, trying to use 'the best' and 'my favorite'.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Show & Share: My Neighborhood Favorites" - Paired sharing activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each student gets a simple handout with a drawing space for "My Favorite Neighborhood Place" and two sentence frames: "My favorite place is ________. It is the best because _________." and "I like _________ because _________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students first draw their favorite place in the neighborhood (e.g., park, playground, a specific street with beautiful flowers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, in pairs, they take turns showing their drawing and describing their favorite place using the sentence frames. They must explain WHY it is their favorite or the best, using 'because'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner A says: "This is my favorite place, the (park). It is the best because I can (play/see flowers)." or "I like the (street) because it has (big trees)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner B listens, then asks one follow-up question, like "What do you like to do there?" or "Is it fun?". Then, Partner B shares their drawing and description.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, listening to conversations and providing support for vocabulary and sentence structure, encouraging students to speak freely.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher uses a checklist to note students' ability to:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use 'the best' or 'my favorite'.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use 'because' to explain opinions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speak with intelligible pronunciation (especially /b/ and /f/ sounds).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participate in simple exchanges.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students briefly share with another pair (or the whole class, if time permits) one new thing they learned about their partner's favorite neighborhood place, using the target language. E.g., "Sarah's favorite place is the playground because it is fun."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary and sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'park', 'playground', 'favorite', 'best', 'fun').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of 'because' to justify preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of expression in simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active listening during pair work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small piece of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I liked to talk about today was ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend about my favorite place. Yes / No" (Students circle their answer).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I use today to talk about places?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to tell my partner why I like a place? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What speaking activities worked best to engage students? (e.g., pair interviews, "Show & Share").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to correctly use 'because' to justify their preferences? What adjustments are needed for students struggling with this? (e.g., more sentence frames, flashcards to prompt reasons).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How was the pronunciation of /b/ and /f/? Do I need to provide more explicit modeling for phonemic awareness (onset /b/ and rhyme /ɪg/)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I scaffold for students who are shy about speaking?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will use the vocabulary and sentence structures (superlatives, 'because') practiced today to write simple sentences and paragraphs describing their favorite neighborhood places.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>